mapledmaaddr (#528) -- KILL TABLE 1, POST-TABLE NOTES (a SEPARATE file, so PRETABLE.out's mtime stays the order
evidence).  Written 2026-09-12 after SCORES.txt's KILLTABLE_DONE.

RESULT: 14 legs, 12 exactly on the primary prediction, ZERO escapes.  Restore cmp-CLEAN, binary FRESH, sentinel cleared.
The frozen instrument was `cmp'-IDENTICAL to regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py at launch (SCORES.txt header).

    BASE         16/16 CONTROL=OK   exact
    HEAD          5/16              exact -- eleven failing; `other read' passes (it never reads DMAADDR)
    M_KEEPFATAL  15/16              exact: read silent ALONE
    M_REWORD     15/16              exact: read silent ALONE
    M_LATCHED    15/16              exact: read silent ALONE
    M_EXITONLY    6/16              exact
    M_RET0        7/16              the PRETABLE's STATED ALTERNATE, not its primary 8/16 (below)
    M_DECLINE     9/16              exact
    M_HOIST      15/16              exact: other read ALONE
    M_READCLEAR  15/16              exact: double read 2 ALONE
    M_EQ4        15/16              exact: read four ALONE
    M_BIT30      15/16              exact: read all ones ALONE
    M_ZEROEXIT   13/16              PREDICTED 15/16 -- killed by three rows, not one (below)
    M_MASKLOW     9/16              exact

THE TWO MISSES, stated as what they are.
 * M_RET0.  The PRETABLE predicted 8/16 with `read silent' passing, and wrote down its own uncertainty: "the
   failed-access message is debug(), invisible here -- a PREDICTION on verbosity, and if it is wrong read silent reds
   too: 7/16".  It was wrong, and the stated alternate is what measured.  The mechanism is in the tree and in the
   carrier: memory_rw.c's failed-device message is `debug("[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]")', naming
   `dreamcast_maple', and single-stepping raises debug() above its threshold -- so under `step' it prints.  A bonus,
   measured: the silence row ALSO sees the FAULT idiom, independently of the poisoned value rows.
 * M_ZEROEXIT.  Predicted `initial zero' as its sole killer; it also killed `marker survive' and `exit status'.  My
   session analysis was wrong: the survive session reads MAPLE_DMAADDR on a FRESH device -- no write precedes it -- so
   it reads zero and exits too.  The kill is real and stronger than predicted; the "sole killer" claim for this leg was
   an error in the prediction, not a probe behaviour.  The other four value-keyed legs (HOIST, READCLEAR, EQ4, BIT30)
   each died on exactly the row the ruling assigned.

THE DISCRIMINATING CLAIM, as corrected: the three announce legs die on `read silent' alone; M_HOIST on `other read'
alone; M_READCLEAR on `double read 2' alone; M_EQ4 on `read four' alone; M_BIT30 on `read all ones' alone.
